Fast determinantal sampling on general spaces and diffusion geometry Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license arXiv:2607.06644v1 [stat.ML] 07 Jul 2026 Fast determinantal sampling on general spaces and diffusion geometry Hoang-Son Tran Department of Mathematics National University of Singapore transonsp97@gmail.com and Pranav Gupta Department of Mathematics National University of Singapore pranav.gupta@u.nus.edu and Subhroshekhar Ghosh Department of Mathematics National University of Singapore subhrowork@gmail.com † † thanks: Corresponding author Abstract Determinantal point processes (DPPs) have recently emerged as a kernel-based alternative to standard independent sampling for constructing efficient minibatches, coresets, and other compact representations of large-scale datasets. In particular, sampling mechanisms based on DPPs are believed to demonstrate better approximation properties compared to classical i.i.d. samplers, even at the scale of the exponent. One of the key strengths of DPP based samplers is that they can be deployed over very general spaces, in contrast to more classical sampling methods beyond i.i.d. which tend to work in very well-structured settings, principally Euclidean spaces. In this work, we establish explicit rate guarantees for determinantal sampling in spaces that extend far beyond known Euclidean setups, focussing on spectral kernels obtained from eigenspaces of naturally associated Laplacian and other Markov diffusion operators. This includes, in particular, Riemannian manifolds and weighted networks. In determinantal sampling from compact Riemannian manifolds, we establish sampling rates that automatically pick up the intrinsic dimensionality d int d_{	ext{int}} of the underlying manifold (as opposed to the ambient dimension of the data, which is usually much larger). In the setting of networks, we investigate DPP-based samplers on the celebrated k-nearest neighbour graphs, as well as weighted random geometric graphs, and demonstrate a similar improved dependence on the intrinsic dimensionality of the data. Overall, our approach achieves guarantees of ( sample size ) − 1 2 − 1 2 ​ d int  ig(	ext{sample size} ig)^{- rac{1}{2}- rac{1}{2d_{	ext{int}}}} that match known rates on Euclidean spaces of comparable dimension. In terms of techniques, we connect to the celebrated Weyl’s Law for manifold spectra, and leverage tools from the theory of Markov diffusions and Dirichlet forms as well as certain ingredients from the theory of pseudodifferential operators, which could be of independent interest in this area. 1 Introduction 1.1 Motivation 1.1.1 Minibatches and quadratures Minibatches in machine learning. Let 𝒳 = { x 1 , … , x N } \mathcal{X}=\{x_{1},\ldots,x_{N}\} be a finite data set, and let ℱ \mathcal{F} be a family of real-valued functions f : 𝒳 → ℝ f:\mathcal{X}	o\mathbb{R} , which we call queries on 𝒳 \mathcal{X} . In many tasks in machine learning, one is interested in statistics of the form L ⁡ ( f ) := 1 N ​ ∑ i = 1 N f ⁡ ( x i ) , f ∈ ℱ . L(f):= rac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}f(x_{i}),\qquad f\in\mathcal{F}. For instance, f ⁡ ( x i ) f(x_{i}) may represent the loss, a gradient coordinate, or a feature-dependent quantity associated with the data point x i x_{i} . When N N is large, evaluating L ⁡ ( f ) L(f) exactly can be computationally expensive. A standard approach is therefore to sample a much smaller collection 𝒮 ⊂ 𝒳 \mathcal{S}\subset\mathcal{X} , called a minibatch , with cardinality n ≪ N n\ll N , and to approximate L ⁡ ( f ) L(f) by a weighted empirical average L 𝒮 , w ​ ( f ) := 1 n ​ ∑ x ∈ 𝒮 w ⁡ ( x ) ​ f ​ ( x ) , f ∈ ℱ , L_{\mathcal{S},w}(f):= rac{1}{n}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{S}}w(x)f(x),\qquad f\in\mathcal{F}, where w : 𝒳 → ℝ w:\mathcal{X}	o\mathbb{R} is a suitable weight function. Given an accuracy parameter ε > 0 \varepsilon>0 , one would like to design a sampling scheme for 𝒮 \mathcal{S} , together with weights w w , such that with high probability sup f ∈ ℱ | L 𝒮 , w ​ ( f ) − L ⁡ ( f ) | ≤ ε . \sup_{f\in\mathcal{F}}|L_{\mathcal{S},w}(f)-L(f)|\leq\varepsilon. A fundamental problem in minibatch sampling is to achieve such a guarantee while keeping the minibatch size n n as small as possible. Quadrature rules for Monte Carlo integration. A closely related problem is the construction of quadrature rules for Monte Carlo integration. Let μ \mu be a probability measure on ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} , for instance of the form μ ⁡ ( d ​ x ) = ρ ⁡ ( x ) ​ d ​ x \mu(\mathrm{d}x)=ho(x)\mathrm{d}x . Given a class ℱ \mathcal{F} of test functions on ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} , for example Lipschitz or smooth functions, one is interested in approximating L ⁡ ( f ) := ∫ ℝ d f ⁡ ( x ) ​ μ ​ ( 𝑑 x ) , f ∈ ℱ . L(f):=\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}f(x)\mu(\mathrm{d}x),\qquad f\in\mathcal{F}. A quadrature rule produces nodes X 1 , … , X n ∈ ℝ d X_{1},\ldots,X_{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{d} , together with weights, and approximates L ⁡ ( f ) L(f) by L 𝒮 , w ​ ( f ) := 1 n ​ ∑ i = 1 n w ⁡ ( X i ) ​ f ​ ( X i ) , 𝒮 = { X 1 , … , X n } . L_{\mathcal{S},w}(f):= rac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}w(X_{i})f(X_{i}),\qquad\mathcal{S}=\{X_{1},\ldots,X_{n}\}. The performance of such a rule may be measured, for a fixed test function f f , by the mean-square error 𝔼 ⁡ [ | L 𝒮 , w ​ ( f ) − L ⁡ ( f ) | 2 ] \mathbb{E}[|L_{\mathcal{S},w}(f)-L(f)|^{2}] . In the Monte Carlo setting, the central question is how fast this error decays as the number of nodes n n tends to infinity. A unified viewpoint. The minibatch and quadrature problems can be viewed through the same lens. In both cases, there is a probability measure μ \mu on a space 𝒳 \mathcal{X} , together with a class ℱ \mathcal{F} of test functions on 𝒳 \mathcal{X} , and the goal is to approximate the linear functional L ⁡ ( f ) = ∫ 𝒳 f ⁡ ( x ) ​ μ ​ ( 𝑑 x ) , f ∈ ℱ , L(f)=\int_{\mathcal{X}}f(x)\mu(\mathrm{d}x),\qquad f\in\mathcal{F}, by evaluating the functions only at a small number of selected points. Thus, the common objective is to construct a small weighted point configuration 𝒮 ⊂ 𝒳 \mathcal{S}\subset\mathcal{X} such that L 𝒮 , w ​ ( f ) := 1 n ​ ∑ x ∈ 𝒮 w ⁡ ( x ) ​ f ​ ( x ) ≈ L ⁡ ( f ) simultaneously for many ​ f ∈ ℱ , L_{\mathcal{S},w}(f):= rac{1}{n}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{S}}w(x)f(x)\approx L(f)\quad	ext{simultaneously for many }f\in\mathcal{F}, where n n is the sample size and w w is a suitable weight function. From this perspective, minibatch sampling and quadrature construction are both instances of the same approximation problem: replacing a reference probability measure by a small weighted discrete measure while controlling integration errors over a prescribed function class. 1.1.2 Importance sampling as a benchmark. A common independent-sampling benchmark for both minibatch sampling and quadrature is importance sampling . In the unified formulation, let μ \mu be the target probability measure on 𝒳 \mathcal{X} , and let ν 
u be a proposal probability measure, which is often chosen such that μ \mu is absolutely continuous with respect to ν 
