al quadratures [ 4 , 5 , 8 ] , minibatches in stochastic gradient descent [ 2 ] or building coresets [ 22 , 3 , 15 ] . Roughly speaking, DPP-based methods can yield MSE bounds of order n − 1 − η n^{-1-\eta} for some η > 0 \eta>0 (typically, η = 1 / d \eta=1/d where d d is the dimension of data). This is a strict improvement over the MSE rate of importance sampling, which is of order n − 1 n^{-1} . A key mechanism behind this improvement is the variance reduction property of linear statistics of DPPs, which will be discussed in detail in the next section. 1.2 Variance reduction phenomenon 1.2.1 Linear statistics and variance reduction phenomenon A point process 𝒮 \mathcal{S} on a space 𝒳 \mathcal{X} is a random configuration of discrete points in 𝒳 \mathcal{X} . In general, the cardinality of 𝒮 \mathcal{S} may be random, or even infinite. In this paper, however, we restrict our attention to point processes with fixed cardinality, say | 𝒮 | = n |\mathcal{S}|=n almost surely. For a measurable function f : 𝒳 → ℝ f:\mathcal{X}	o\mathbb{R} , the linear statistic of 𝒮 \mathcal{S} associated with f f is defined by 𝒮 ⁡ ( f ) := ∑ x ∈ 𝒮 f ⁡ ( x ) . \mathcal{S}(f):=\sum_{x\in\mathcal{S}}f(x). Linear statistics play a central role in our sampling setting because of the simple identity L 𝒮 , w ​ ( f ) = 1 n ​ ∑ x ∈ 𝒮 w ⁡ ( x ) ​ f ​ ( x ) = 1 n ​ 𝒮 ​ ( w ​ f ) . L_{\mathcal{S},w}(f)= rac{1}{n}\sum_{x\in\mathcal{S}}w(x)f(x)= rac{1}{n}\mathcal{S}(wf). Thus the weighted estimator L 𝒮 , w ​ ( f ) L_{\mathcal{S},w}(f) is, up to the normalization factor 1 / n 1/n , a linear statistic of the point process 𝒮 \mathcal{S} with test function w ​ f wf . Consequently, understanding the mean-square error of L 𝒮 , w ​ ( f ) L_{\mathcal{S},w}(f) naturally leads to the study of variances of linear statistics. If 𝒮 = { X 1 , … , X n } \mathcal{S}=\{X_{1},\ldots,X_{n}\} , then 𝒮 ⁡ ( f ) = ∑ i = 1 n f ⁡ ( X i ) \mathcal{S}(f)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}f(X_{i}) . For independent samples X 1 , … , X n X_{1},\ldo