}_{n}(f)]=O(n^{1-2\alpha/d}). For α = 1 \alpha=1 , this gives the rate O ⁡ ( n 1 − 2 / d ) O(n^{1-2/d}) , improving the exponent obtained for m-OPEs in the corresponding Lipschitz or first-order regularity regime. 1.2.3 Between continuous and discrete DPPs For DPPs on discrete data sets, often referred to as discrete DPPs , variance reduction is much less understood. A careful inspection of the known examples on ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} reveals that the analytic and geometric structures of the background space play an essential role. By contrast, for discrete DPPs, the underlying space is only a finite cloud of points and may not carry enough intrinsic analytic structure to support direct arguments. On the other hand, discrete DPPs are of fundamental importance because of their applications to minibatch and coreset sampling. Understanding variance reduction for such processes is therefore a natural and important problem. In [ 21 ] , the authors provide a general discretization framework that transfers DPPs from a continuous space to a finite data set. Roughly speaking, given a continuous DPP on an underlying space, such as ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} , the framework constructs a discrete DPP supported on the data set whose variance decay matches that of the original continuous process, up to an explicit error depending on the minibatch size n n , the ambient data set size N N , and the failure probability δ \delta . Consequently, when N N is sufficiently large, the discretized DPP inherits essentially the same variance-reduction rate as its continuous counterpart. On a related note, Jaquard and Keriven [ 15 ] also study the relationship between discrete and continuous DPPs. In particular, they introduce the notion of statistical consistency of discrete-to-continuous DPPs , which, roughly speaking, provides conditions under which discrete DPPs asymptotically behave like their continuous counterparts on the underlying space. As applications, they demonstrate their framework in several settings, including the convergence of discrete OPEs to continuous OPEs, and the convergence of discrete harmonic ensembles on Gaussian geometric random graphs to harmonic ensembles on manifolds. These results suggest that such discrete DPP models may asymptotically inherit the sampling properties of the corresponding continuous models on the underlying space. However, explicit sampling rates for these models, for instance for harmonic ensembles and their discrete counterparts on geometric random graphs, are not addressed in [ 15 ] , and call for further investigation. Such rates are crucial for the theoretical guarantees on repulsive sampling methods, such as DPPs, in practical applications. In particular, it is of fundamental importance to understand how these rates depend on intrinsic geometric features of the underlying space, how they compare with existing guarantees on Euclidean domains, and how they compare with independent sampling schemes such as importance sampling. 1.3 Open problems and our contributions Questions and challenges. The preceding discussion motivates the search for new classes of DPPs that enjoy variance reduction. A common feature of both the multivariate OPE construction and the wavelet-DPP construction is that they rely strongly on the Euclidean structure of the background space ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} . In particular, the reference measure μ \mu is typically assumed to be full-dimensional: it has a density with respect to Lebesgue measure on ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} , and this density is positive on a nonempty open subset. Moreover, the resulting variance decay rates are governed by the ambient Euclidean dimension d d ; for example, a mean-square error rate of the form n − 1 − η n^{-1-\eta} typically has η \eta of order 1 / d 1/d . This dependence on the ambient dimension is a significant limitation in modern high-dimensional applications, where data often live in a very high-dimensional Euclidean space, but their effective or intrinsic dimension may be much smaller. This is the viewpoint behind the manifold hypothesis , which posits that high-dimensional data are concentrated near, or on, a low-dimensional manifold ℳ ⊂ ℝ d \mathcal{M}\subset\mathbb{R}^{d} . In such settings, constructions based directly on full-dimensional Lebesgue geometry are no longer well adapted: the reference measure may be singular with respect to Lebesgue measure on ℝ d \mathbb{R}^{d} , and the relevant dimension should be the intrinsic dimension of ℳ \mathcal{M} , rather than the ambient dimension d d . This leads to a natural question: can one construct DPP samplers with provable variance reduction whose rates are governed by the intrinsic geometry of the data rather than by an ambient Euclidean structure? More broadly, can one develop DPP-based sampling schemes on curved spaces, graphs, or other non-Euclidean domains, where the appropriate notion of dimension is encoded by the spectral geometry of the underlying space? Our contributions. In this article, we address this question by introducing a new class of DPPs constructed from the eigenfunctions of a Laplacian-type operator L \mathrm{L} . This framework includes, for instance, the Laplace–Beltrami operator on a Riemannian manifold and graph Laplacians on weighted graphs. The resulting DPPs are therefore intrinsically defined: their construction depends on the geometry of the underlying space through the operator L \mathrm{L} , rather than on an ambient Euclidean coordinate system. Our main technical result is a master theorem that bounds the variance of linear statistics of these spectral DPPs in terms of the eigenvalue distribution of L \mathrm{L} . This connects the variance reduction problem for DPPs to spectral asymptotics, and in particular to Weyl-type laws. As a consequence, whenever the eigenvalues of L \mathrm{L} obey an appropriate Weyl law, the associated DPP exhibits variance reduction at a rate determined by the corresponding spectral dimension. This yields concrete applications in several settings. In Section 4 , we present our first application: Monte Carlo integration on compact Riemannian manifolds. In this setting, we construct DPP-based quadrature rules for Lipschitz test functions, with MSE rates roughly of order n − 1 − 1 / m n^{-1-1/m} , governed by the manifold dimension m m . In Section 5 , we turn to minibatch sampling from data sets whose points arise from low-dimensional geometric structures, for instance when the data are supported on a submanifold. Our sampling scheme first builds a geometric random graph on the data points, and then samples a minibatch from a DPP whose kernel is constructed from eigenvectors of the graph Laplacian. We demonstrate the robustness of this approach by focusing on important classes of weighted random geometric graphs, including k k -nearest neighbor graphs ( k k -NN). For these models, we provide rigorous theoretical guarantees showing that the accuracy rate ε \varepsilon is roughly of order n − 1 / 2 − 1 / ( 2 m ) n^{-1/2-1/(2m)} , where m m is the intrinsic dimension of the data, rather than the ambient Euclidean dimension d d . Together, these results provide a spectral and intrinsic approach to DPP-based variance reduction beyond the Euclidean full-dimensional setting. 2 Background 2.1 Determinantal point processes Let 𝒳 \mathcal{X} be a Polish space equipped with a reference measure μ \mu , where typical examples include Euclidean spaces, Riemannian manifolds, and discrete spaces such as graphs. A point process 𝒮 \mathcal{S} on 𝒳 \mathcal{X} is a random locally finite subset of 𝒳 \mathcal{X} . We say that 𝒮 \mathcal{S} is a determinantal point process (DPP) with respect to μ \mu if there exists a measurable function K : 𝒳 × 𝒳 → ℂ K:\mathcal{X}	imes\mathcal{X}	o\mathbb{C} such that, for every k ∈ ℕ k\in\mathbb{N} and every bounded measurable function f : 𝒳 k → ℝ f:\mathcal{X}^{k}	o\mathbb{R} , 𝔼 ⁡ [ ∑ ≠ f ⁡ ( x i 1 , … , x i k ) ] = ∫ 𝒳 k f ⁡ ( x 1 , … , x k ) ​ det [ K ⁡ (